{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3169","artifact_type":"feature-context","name":"plan/feature-context-duplicate-detection-content-match.md@sha256:0605d6c52d785f26724eb08e12667bab24618f83abbee512c81b29302466bcd0"},"owner_reference":"3169","content":"# Feature Context \u2014 Duplicate detection that matches content, not title characters\n\n**Item**: #3169 (P1, type:bug)\n**Artifact**: `plan/feature-context-duplicate-detection-content-match.md`\n**Scope of this document**: WHAT and WHY only. No implementation design.\n**Replacement Coverage Analysis**: Not applicable \u2014 this is an internal algorithm/timing/cache-freshness defect, not a migration to an external tool.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Problem Space\n\n### 1.1 The need the check exists to serve\n\nBefore a person or agent spends effort researching and planning a problem, they need to be told whether that work already exists, so they can extend the existing item instead of repeating it. Everything below is measured against that need \u2014 not against \"prevent two rows with similar titles\".\n\n### 1.2 The observed failure\n\nOn 2026-08-23 three backlog items were created that duplicated items filed one to three minutes earlier by a concurrent session: #3139 duplicating #3138 (five seconds apart), #3143 duplicating #3141, #3144 duplicating #3140. The duplicate check ran on all three and reported no duplicate. Research and planning had already been spent on each before the check ran at all.\n\n### 1.3 Four independent gaps\n\nEach gap is sufficient on its own to produce the observed failure. Closing any one of them does not close the others.\n\n**(a) It matches the wrong thing.** `_check_for_duplicates()` (`plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:1341`) calls `find_fuzzy_duplicates()` (`plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py:536`), which runs `difflib.SequenceMatcher` over normalised titles at a threshold of `FUZZY_DUPLICATE_THRESHOLD = 0.80` (`backlog_core/models.py:422`). That is character-sequence similarity over exactly one field. The documented failing pair scored 0.4683:\n\n- \"CI check for drift between MCP server tool lists and agent frontmatter `tools:` entries\"\n- \"Agent `tools:` frontmatter drifts from MCP server tool lists with no enforcement and silent failure in both directions\"\n\nThose two titles share most of their *vocabulary* and almost none of their *character sequence*. The failure is not that the matcher lacks semantics \u2014 it is that it compares character order over one short field instead of comparing the words two authors actually used across the whole item.\n\n**(b) It fires at the wrong point in the lifecycle.** The check is invoked from inside `add_item()` at `backlog_core/operations.py:1503` \u2014 the write path. By the time it runs, the research and planning it exists to protect are already spent. Creation is the last step of the workflow, not the first.\n\n**(c) Its result cannot be acted on.** `DuplicateItemError` (`backlog_core/models.py:561`) carries `list[tuple[str, float, str]]` \u2014 `(existing_title, similarity_ratio, file_path)`. A file path is not a handle a caller can pass to any operation to read or extend the existing item. The exception offers exactly two outcomes: abort, or re-run with `force=True`. There is no path that says \"this already exists, go add your findings there\".\n\n**(d) It reads a cache and reports absence as fact.** `_check_for_duplicates()` sources its candidate set from `backend.list_work_items()`. On the GitHub backend (`backlog_core/backends/github_backend.py:178`) that delegates to the reconciliation collaborator and, per its own docstring, lists \"work items from the provider-private cache\" \u2014 no remote consultation. An item written by a concurrent session and not yet pulled is simply invisible, and the check answers \"no duplicate\" rather than \"cannot tell\". This directly contradicts the invariant stated twice in the project's own documentation:\n\n- `plugins/development-harness/AGENTS.md:87` \u2014 \"Cache misses are unavailable data, not authoritative empty results\"\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md:436` \u2014 \"A missing cache record is reported as unavailable data, never as an authoritative empty result.\"\n\n### 1.4 A fifth gap: the documented check has no CLI implementation\n\nThe create workflow's Step 3 (`skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/start.md:87`\u2013`109`) already specifies content-scoped detection *before* the write:\n\n```\nbacklog_list(search=\"{concept1} OR {concept2} OR {concept3}\", match_context=true)\n```\n\nThat instruction is MCP-only. The CLI `backlog list` command exposes no `--search`; the only `--search` option in `sam_schema/backlog.py` is on the unrelated `merged_prs` command (`sam_schema/backlog.py:344`). The CLI's `--title` is substring matching on one field \u2014 the same class of check as the fuzzy-title gate this item is about.\n\nThis matters because the CLI is the documented fallback when the MCP write is unavailable, and that is exactly what happened in the observed incident: the MCP `backlog_add` call was rejected, the caller fell back to the CLI, and on that path the documented pre-write check had no implementation to call. The same workflow describes MCP and CLI as \"two equivalent interfaces to the same underlying `add_item()` operation\" \u2014 true for creation, false for the detection that must precede it, and nothing on the CLI path says so.\n\n### 1.5 The capability already exists \u2014 but not where the gate can reach it\n\n`backlog_list`'s full-text search matches across title, description, acceptance criteria and every section body, with OR/AND/NOT operators, parenthetical grouping, regex, field-scoped terms, and `match_context` reporting where each term matched. It is unused by the duplicate gate.\n\n**Structural constraint (verified, and material to scoping):** that search engine lives in the MCP server module \u2014 `_tokenize_search` at `backlog_core/server.py:391`, `_apply_search_filter` at `backlog_core/server.py:516`, applied at `backlog_core/server.py:2166` \u2014 and operates on already-serialised list entries (`list[dict[str, str | bool]]`), not on `BacklogItem`. `add_item()` and the CLI both sit *below* that layer, in `backlog_core/operations.py`. So \"the capability exists in the same package\" is true, but it is not currently reachable from either the write gate or the CLI without moving it. This is a fact about the current shape of the code, not a prescription for how to fix it.\n\nBy contrast, the freshness half *is* already reachable at the operations layer: `refresh_local_cache_from_github()` exists at `backlog_core/operations.py:1572` and `list_items(from_github=True)` calls it at `operations.py:1806`. The CLI already surfaces this as `--refresh`. Both default to off.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Why This Matters\n\n1. **Wasted, unrecoverable effort.** The cost of a missed duplicate is not a redundant row \u2014 it is the research and planning spent twice, plus the later reconciliation of two items describing one problem. Three occurrences in a single day is the measured rate under concurrent sessions.\n\n2. **A documented invariant is being violated in production.** Gap (d) is not a design preference; the project states the opposite behaviour as an invariant in two separate documents. A read path that reports cache absence as authoritative emptiness is a defect against the project's own stated contract.\n\n3. **Documentation and implementation have already diverged.** `create/start.md` Step 3 and `docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` (lines 126, 154, 177) both describe search-based detection at Step 3. The implementation runs title-only `SequenceMatcher`. Whichever direction this is resolved, it must be resolved \u2014 a workflow doc that instructs an agent to perform a check the CLI cannot perform is a live source of the exact failure observed.\n\n4. **The failure mode is silent and confidence-inspiring.** The check runs, returns clean, and the caller reasonably concludes the work is new. A check that cannot distinguish \"nothing matches\" from \"could not look\" is worse than no check, because it manufactures false confidence at precisely the moment the caller is deciding whether to invest.\n\n5. **Concurrency is the normal operating mode.** Multi-session and swarm workflows are standard here. A duplicate gate that only sees data one session has already pulled is structurally unable to serve that mode.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Desired Outcomes\n\nTaken as-groomed from the item's Acceptance Criteria \u2014 restated here as outcomes, not redefined:\n\n| # | Outcome | Serves gap |\n|---|---------|-----------|\n| DO-1 | Given a new item whose **title differs** from an existing item's but whose description / acceptance criteria / section content describes the same problem, the check flags the existing item as a probable duplicate. | (a) |\n| DO-2 | Given a new item created shortly after a concurrent session's same-topic item that the local cache has not yet observed, the check **either detects it or explicitly reports that it could not verify** \u2014 never silently reports \"no duplicate\" from stale data. | (d) |\n| DO-3 | When a duplicate is flagged, the caller receives an **actionable item reference/handle** they can open and extend \u2014 not only a file path. | (c) |\n| DO-4 | The check runs and produces a result **via the CLI `backlog add` path**, not only via MCP. | fifth gap |\n| DO-5 | **No increase in false-positive rejections** for genuinely non-duplicate items (dissimilar titles *and* dissimilar content). | regression guard |\n\nTwo outcomes named in the item's Description are worth carrying forward explicitly even though they are not separately enumerated as acceptance criteria:\n\n- **DO-6 (timing, gap (b))**: the answer should reach the caller *before* effort is spent, not at write time. Note that DO-4 as written anchors the check to the `backlog add` path, which is still the write path \u2014 see Open Question OQ-1.\n- **DO-7 (interface honesty)**: MCP and CLI are documented as equivalent. Either they become equivalent for detection, or the documentation stops claiming they are. Silent non-equivalence between two interfaces documented as equivalent is a defect either way.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Stakeholders\n\n| Stakeholder | Stake | What \"done\" looks like to them |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Agents creating items via MCP** (`backlog_add`) | Primary victim of the observed incident. | The tool tells them the work exists, with a reference, before they finish composing. |\n| **Agents creating items via CLI** (`backlog add`, dispatch/scripting/MCP-fallback contexts) | Currently have *no* content-based check at all. | The CLI path is no longer a silent hole in the gate. |\n| **Concurrent / swarm sessions** | Generate the exact race that defeats the cache read. | Their writes are visible to each other's checks, or the checks say they can't tell. |\n| **The `/dh:work-backlog-item create` orchestrator** | Executes `create/start.md` Step 3 literally. | The instruction it is given is executable on every path it can be invoked from. |\n| **Backlog maintainers / triagers** | Absorb the cost of merging duplicate items after the fact. | Fewer duplicate pairs reaching the backlog; existing items get extended instead. |\n| **Backend implementers** (GitHub, SQLite, Memory, Beads) | Share the `list_work_items()` protocol contract (`backlog_core/backend_types.py:141`). | Any contract change keeps native-storage backends valid; only the cache-composing remote backend has the staleness class. |\n| **Documentation & generated workflow graph** | `create/start.md`, `docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md`, `skills/backlog/SKILL.md`, and the generated `dh-workflow-graph.json` / `L1` / `G8` layers all mirror Step 3. | They describe what the code does, and the generated artifacts are refreshed rather than left stale. |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Risks\n\n| ID | Risk | Why it matters here |\n|---|---|---|\n| R-1 | **False positives rise.** Widening from one short field to full item body widens the match surface substantially. Two unrelated items in the same subsystem share a lot of vocabulary. | DO-5 is an explicit acceptance criterion, and a gate that cries wolf gets bypassed with `force=True` reflexively, restoring the original failure with extra steps. |\n| R-2 | **Latency and cost on the write path.** Consulting the remote to satisfy DO-2 turns a local read into a network round-trip on every `add`. | `add_item` is called in loops by dispatch/scripting contexts. A slow or rate-limited gate is a gate that gets disabled. |\n| R-3 | **\"Could not verify\" becomes noise.** If the unverifiable case is common (offline, rate-limited, no GitHub configured), DO-2's honest signal degrades into a warning everyone ignores. | The invariant is satisfied in letter while the need is unserved. |\n| R-4 | **Layering.** The search engine sits in the MCP server module above `operations.py` (\u00a71.5). Making it reachable from both `add_item` and the CLI is a structural move, not a local edit. | Scope and blast radius are larger than the four-line matcher swap the title implies. |\n| R-5 | **Test lock-in.** Roughly 24 existing tests across three classes are written against current behaviour: `TestFindFuzzyDuplicates` + `TestFindFuzzyDuplicatesEdgeCases` (~14, title-only algorithm), `TestAddItemDuplicateDetection` (3, write-time raise), `TestDuplicateItemError` (7, exception shape). | Changing the result shape or timing invalidates them; rewriting them is part of the work, and a \"no false-positive increase\" claim (DO-5) needs a test that can actually measure it. |\n| R-6 | **Backend contract fan-out.** `list_work_items()` is a protocol method on four backends. | A freshness parameter or staleness signal added to it touches implementations with no cache and no staleness class. |\n| R-7 | **Doc/graph drift.** Step 3 is mirrored in `docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` and in generated artifacts (`docs/dh-workflow-graph.json`, `workflow-layers/L1-trace-work-backlog-item.json`, `workflow-layers/G8-backend-routing.json`). | Changing the mechanism without refreshing them re-creates the doc/implementation mismatch this item exists to close. |\n| R-8 | **Partial fix looks like a fix.** The four gaps are independent. Fixing the matcher alone still misses a concurrent write; fixing freshness alone still misses the reworded title. | Highest risk to the outcome: the item is closed after one gap is closed and the failure recurs. |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Open Questions\n\n**OQ-1 \u2014 Where does the check actually belong?** The Description argues the check must run \"before effort is spent \u2026 at the point work is picked up\" (gap (b)), but DO-4 anchors it to the `backlog add` path, which is still the write. Are these one change or two \u2014 a corrected write-time gate *plus* a separate earlier advisory check at work-pickup time? Scope depends on the answer.\n\n**OQ-2 \u2014 What is the acceptance bar for \"same problem, different words\"?** `create/start.md:89` states plainly: \"Duplicate detection uses boolean substring matching, not semantic matching.\" The documented failing pair shares heavy vocabulary and would likely be caught by content-scoped substring/token matching. Does DO-1 require only *content-scoped lexical* matching (achievable with the existing engine), or true semantic equivalence for a pair sharing no vocabulary at all (a different class of capability)? These have very different costs.\n\n**OQ-3 \u2014 What counts as \"sufficiently fresh\"?** DO-2 permits either detection or an honest \"could not verify\". Is the intent: always refresh before answering; refresh only when the cache is older than some age; or never refresh and always disclose staleness? R-2 and R-3 trade directly against each other here.\n\n**OQ-4 \u2014 What is the shape of the answer?** DO-3 requires an actionable reference. Is `DuplicateItemError` reshaped to carry item references, or does the check stop raising and start *returning* a result the caller inspects? Raising forbids \"found, and here is enough content to decide\"; the Description says the caller needs \"the existing item's reference **and enough of its content to judge**\".\n\n**OQ-5 \u2014 Is `force` still the right escape hatch?** With DO-3, the caller has a third option (extend the existing item). Does `force=True` retain \"skip the check\", or become \"I looked and it is not a duplicate\"? Both MCP (`backlog_add`) and CLI (`add`) document `force` as \"Skip fuzzy duplicate check\" today.\n\n**OQ-6 \u2014 What happens to `find_fuzzy_duplicates`?** Removed, or retained as a fallback when the content search or a fresh read is unavailable? This is the difference between a replacement and an addition, and it determines the fate of ~14 tests.\n\n**OQ-7 \u2014 Which backends must honour the freshness contract?** Only the cache-composing GitHub backend has this staleness class. Do SQLite / Memory / Beads need contract changes, or only compatibility verification?\n\n**OQ-8 \u2014 How is DO-5 measured?** \"No increase in false-positive rejections\" is only checkable against a baseline. Is there a corpus of known non-duplicate pairs to measure against, or does this become a judgement call at review time?\n\n---\n\n## 7. Out of Scope (noted, not proposed)\n\n- `_resolve_reference()` (`backlog_core/operations.py:1359`) performs a second cache-only `backend.list_work_items()` read at write time for reference-uniquing. Same read pattern, same staleness class, different purpose. Recorded because it shows the pattern recurs; not part of this item.\n- `backlog_close(reason=\"duplicate\")` is an unrelated mechanism (post-hoc dismissal, not detection) despite the shared word.\n- CI concurrency configuration in `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` matched the word \"duplicate\" during impact analysis and is unrelated.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Verification Notes\n\nAll code and documentation citations in \u00a71 and \u00a75 were read directly from the working tree during this research, not carried over from the grooming summary. Two corrections to prior grooming output are recorded in the concerns returned alongside this artifact: the `skills/backlog/SKILL.md` line citations in the Impact Radius section do not resolve, and `docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` is internally ambiguous about whether Step 3 is content-scoped or title-scoped.\n"}